# Android CI cost

Sibling of [android-app](android-app.mdc) and [release-build](release-build.mdc).
On-device install and verification is the
[android-deploy](../../skills/android-deploy/SKILL.md) skill.

An Android build is slow, and packaging is the slow half. A workflow that runs
`assembleDebug` on every push spends the account's Actions allowance producing an
artifact that, in practice, nobody downloads — the APK that gets installed is
almost always the one built locally and pushed over adb.

## Don't package an APK in CI unless something consumes it

```yaml
# Wrong when nothing downloads the artifact: pays for DEX + packaging on every push.
- run: ./gradlew testDebugUnitTest --no-daemon
- run: ./gradlew assembleDebug --no-daemon
- uses: actions/upload-artifact@v4
  with: { name: debug-apk, path: app/build/outputs/apk/debug/*.apk }
```

```yaml
# Right for a team that installs from a local build.
- run: ./gradlew testDebugUnitTest --no-daemon
- uses: actions/upload-artifact@v4        # only on failure, and only the report
  if: failure()
  with: { name: unit-test-report, path: app/build/reports/tests/ }
```

Keep the packaging step only where it earns its cost: a tag/release workflow, a
distribution channel (Firebase App Distribution, Play internal track), or a QA
team that genuinely pulls the artifact. Then run it on `push: tags` or
`workflow_dispatch`, not on every commit.

## You lose less than it looks — but know precisely what

Dropping `assembleDebug` does **not** stop CI from catching a broken build,
because the test task already depends on most of the pipeline.

Verified with `gradlew testDebugUnitTest --dry-run` on an AGP 8.7.2 project — the
task graph includes:

```
:app:processDebugMainManifest
:app:processDebugManifestForPackage
:app:processDebugResources
:app:mergeDebugResources
:app:compileDebugKotlin
```

and contains **no** `dexBuilderDebug`, `packageDebug` or `assembleDebug`.

So a Kotlin compile error, a malformed `AndroidManifest.xml` and a missing or
misnamed resource all still fail CI. What is no longer exercised:

- **DEX** — desugaring, and the 64K method limit
- **Packaging and signing** — anything that only shows up in an assembled APK
- **R8** — already release-only, and the failures it causes are silent rather
  than loud, so CI was never the place they surfaced. See
  [release-build](release-build.mdc).

Say this in a comment in the workflow. A future reader who sees tests but no
build will otherwise "fix" it by adding `assembleDebug` back.

## Cheap wins that don't cost coverage

- **Fail fast on the cheapest signal.** Unit tests before anything that packages.
- **Upload artifacts on `if: failure()` only.** A test report on every green run
  is storage and minutes for something nobody opens.
- **Don't pin a Gradle version in the action** — let the wrapper decide, so CI
  and local builds cannot drift onto different Gradle versions and re-download a
  second distribution.
- **One JDK, matching the project.** AGP 8.x wants JDK 17–21; setting a newer one
  costs a download and then fails (see below).
- Reserve matrix builds for something that genuinely varies by axis. An Android
  unit-test suite rarely does.

## AGP and the JDK: the error is a bare version number

Worth pinning in CI *and* documenting for local builds. AGP 8.7.x runs on JDK
17–21. Given a newer JDK, Gradle refuses with no explanation beyond the version
it found:

```
FAILURE: Build failed with an exception.
* What went wrong:
25.0.2
```

Observed with Gradle 8.13 under the JDK 25 that Android Studio was shipping as
its bundled JBR. Nothing in that message says "unsupported Java version", so it
reads as a corrupt install.

`actions/setup-java` makes this a non-issue in CI, which is exactly why it bites
locally instead — a repo whose CI is green can still be unbuildable on a fresh
machine. Name the required JDK in the repo's `CLAUDE.md`/README rather than
leaving the next person to decode a version number, and do not assume the
bundled JBR is a usable JDK.

### The fix: `org.gradle.java.home`, not a different `JAVA_HOME`

Naming the JDK is not enough when `JAVA_HOME` points at the JBR and you cannot
change it (another tool on the machine needs it). Gradle's launcher can stay on
the newer JDK while the daemon, which does the compiling, runs on a supported
one. Verified Oct-19-2026 on a copy of a real project (AGP 8.7, Gradle 8.13,
`JAVA_HOME` = Android Studio's JBR, JDK 25.0.3):

| Run | Result |
|---|---|
| no `org.gradle.java.home` | `BUILD FAILED in 10s`, message is just `25.0.3` |
| `org.gradle.java.home=<JDK 17 path>` | `BUILD SUCCESSFUL in 20s` |

The stack trace shows where it dies: `JavaVersion.parse` called from
`compileKotlinScriptToDirectory`, which is Gradle's own embedded build-script
compiler, not your code and not AGP. That is why it fails before any task runs.

Put the property in the **per-user** `~/.gradle/gradle.properties`, not the
project's, because the JDK path is machine-specific and a committed path breaks
every other machine and CI. Confirm which JVM does what:

```
gradlew --version | findstr "JVM"
Launcher JVM:  25.0.3
Daemon JVM:    C:\Program Files\Microsoft\jdk-17...  (from org.gradle.java.home)
```

Not verified here, so do not rely on them: Gradle toolchain resolution as an
alternative, and the daemon-reuse criteria when the two JDKs differ.

## Verify
- [ ] No `assembleDebug`/`assembleRelease` on the default push path unless an
      artifact is actually consumed.
- [ ] Artifact uploads are gated on `if: failure()`, or justified.
- [ ] A comment in the workflow says which coverage the missing package step
      gives up, so it is not "fixed" back in.
- [ ] The JDK version in CI matches what the AGP version supports, and the
      requirement is written down for local builds too.
